# Build context for the dev image (see Dockerfile).
#
# `COPY . /opt/dotfiles` sits directly above the `nix build` that bakes the
# whole home-manager closure into the image, so every file in the context is
# also a cache key: change one and Docker rebuilds ~10GB of closure. It is a
# size cost too - `nix build path:` copies the entire directory into the store,
# so the context lands in the image twice (at /opt/dotfiles and again under
# /nix/store).
#
# What the flake actually reads is flake.nix, flake.lock, lib/, home-manager/,
# darwin/ and secrets/; docker/ is needed separately for the entrypoint and the
# baked CLAUDE.md. Everything excluded below is either unread by the flake or
# rewrites itself without changing what the flake sees.
#
# If a module ever imports from one of these paths, delete that line - the
# failure mode is otherwise confusing: `nix build` reports a missing file that
# is plainly sitting there in your checkout.

# VCS state. Nothing in the image uses it - the flake is fetched with `path:`
# *because* the base image has no git. Both directories rewrite themselves on
# every commit (jj: on every command, including `jj status`), so without this a
# `docker build .` from a working checkout busts the closure layer for changes
# the build cannot see. .jj is excluded from git by .git/info/exclude in a
# colocated repo, not by .gitignore, so it is easy to forget it is even here.
.git
.gitignore
.jj

# Host build outputs. These are symlinks into the *host's* /nix/store and dangle
# inside the image; .direnv additionally holds a whole devShell profile.
**/result
**/result-*
**/.direnv

# Docs, CI and editor config: read by humans, GitHub and VS Code, never by the
# flake. CLAUDE.md is the most frequently edited file in the repo, which is
# exactly why it should not be able to invalidate the closure build. The copy
# that matters inside the container is docker/CLAUDE{,-arm64}.md, baked in as
# Claude's user-level memory; a checkout you actually work on is mounted at
# /work and brings its own.
docs/
README.md
CONCEPTS.md
CLAUDE.md
.github/
.vscode/
.devcontainer.json

# macOS. Patterns here are matched against the whole relative path, not the
# basename, so a bare `.DS_Store` would only ever catch the one at the root -
# hence `**/`.
**/.DS_Store
**/._*
**/.AppleDouble
**/.LSOverride
